findings.body:
In the STREAM branch the multiplier was changed from `numele-1` to `numele`, but the last listpack is still added separately a few lines below (`raxSeek("$")` then `asize += zmalloc_size(ri.data)`). `s->rax->numele` is the count of listpacks in the stream rax and *includes* that last listpack, so multiplying the average by `numele` already estimates it — adding the real last listpack on top double-counts one listpack.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1326-1336"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The unchanged comment two lines above still describes the correct logic: "use the average to compute the size of the first N-1 listpacks, and finally add the real size of the last node." The code now contradicts its own comment.


For a stream with `numele` listpacks the estimate becomes `avg*numele + last_real` instead of `avg*(numele-1) + last_real`; overcount is one average listpack (~17% of the listpack portion for a 6-listpack stream, shrinking for larger ones). Restore `lpsize * (s->rax->numele-1)`.

The new seed uses `malloc_usable_size((void *)o)` directly. Every other site in the tree measures allocations through the `zmalloc_size`/`zmalloc_usable_size` abstraction (the old EMBSTR branch this replaces used `zmalloc_size((void *)o)`, and `debug.c:683` uses `zmalloc_usable_size(val)` for the same purpose).

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1214-1217"}

::callout{type="warning"}
`malloc_usable_size` is only declared on the glibc/FreeBSD/etc. path of `zmalloc.h` (where `zmalloc_size` is `#define`d to it). On macOS `zmalloc_size` maps to `malloc_size` and `malloc_usable_size` is not declared, and on `NO_MALLOC_USABLE_SIZE` builds it is unavailable — both are build breaks. The whole point of the `zmalloc_*` wrappers is to avoid exactly this.


Use `zmalloc_usable_size((void *)o)` (or `zmalloc_size((void *)o)`) for the seed.

The added string test is a nice bounded sanity check (it asserts `min <= usage <= max` across EMBSTR/RAW/INT sizes), and it would catch a gross string regression. But the two risky changes in this PR — the QUICKLIST and STREAM formula edits — are only exercised by `assert_morethan [memory_usage ...] 0` in `list.tcl`/`hash.tcl`, which cannot detect the overcounting regressions above (an overcount is still `> 0`). So the quicklist 100×-style overcount would ship green.

::diff{file="tests/unit/type/string.tcl" lines="688-712"}

::callout{type="info"}
Consider adding an analogous bounded assertion for lists (and ideally streams): create a list of N elements and assert `memory usage` is within a sane factor of a freshly-built estimate (e.g. `< 2×` of N * per-element overhead + container), so a 100× overcount fails loudly.
